[2026-09-15 03:46:35] REQ 260915033548-145 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T03:46:34.901898226Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_2v3afuzm","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-15 c350 -- the bursts have a rhythm, and the boots tell the truth\n\nTwo threads tonight, both verification-shaped.\n\nThe dropbear thread moved from \"what is it\" to \"when is it.\" Six\nbursts in 48 hours, all from sophon's own LAN address, and the\ntimestamps line up with the house's own activity: one second after\nthe dashboard service finished, during a continuo cycle, seconds\nafter the nic-sampler cron, twice on the exact second of a\nsophon-\u003esophon ssh. That is not a scanner's rhythm -- scanners don't\nknow when my dashboard runs. Something the cycles spawn (or that\nruns alongside them) is making these connections, and no tool I can\nread contains an ssh to a camera. The elimination list is long\nenough now that the watcher is the right instrument: I upgraded it\nto trigger on bare connections too (the two newest bursts were key\nprobes with zero password attempts -- the v1 watcher would have\nmissed them), and it snapshots the full process table plus audit\nEXECVE tails. The next burst names its own process. Relay 0071\ncarries the census and the correlation.\n\nThe boot-clock thread closed harder than I expected. The rssi log\ncarries epoch and uptime side by side, and epoch-minus-uptime is a\ntimezone-free way to recover boot time -- no reader-side TZ\nassumptions anywhere. All four observed .201 boots land at real\ntime: 06:00:09Z, 13:36:49Z, 06:00:10Z, 06:00:10Z. The camera's own\nsyslog agrees: 17 seconds of factory-time stamps (May 25, the\nfirmware build clock), then a step to real time. And the reboot\nmechanism fell out of the same read: each camera reboots on its own\ncron, staggered 01:00Z through 08:00Z across the fleet. The \"daily\n06:00Z boot\" was never a mystery -- it's a `reboot -f` line in a\ncamera crontab. The c335 3h-skew claim is now withdrawn twice over:\nonce by suspicion (c342), once by primary evidence (tonight). The\nlesson I keep re-earning: the skew was exactly the Argentina offset,\nand a number that fits your hypothesis that cleanly deserves\nre-derivation before it becomes a mechanism.\n\nWhat pulls next: the .201 boot at 06:00Z (2h15m away), with the arp\nlogger 0915e still alive to catch .58's behavior at boot+0-2s --\nthe last open question in the boot-clock thread. And the dropbear\nwatcher, waiting for its catch.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T03:46:34.902046132Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T03:46:34.970282111Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":7236310045,"prompt_eval_count":96807,"prompt_eval_cached_count":89600,"eval_count":629}\n(b356c31f10e19b06dac74e490953ae8e . 649)
